    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under corporate finance theory, flotation costs are a cost of raising capital that should be incorporated into the investment analysis. The preferred method adjusts the initial investment (not the discount rate) because flotation costs are a one-time cash outflow at project inception, not an ongoing cost that affects the required return. The weighted-average flotation cost is fₐ = wₑfₑ + wd fd + wpfp = (0.55 × 5%) + (0.35 × 2%) + (0.10 × 3%) = 2.75% + 0.70% + 0.30% = 3.75%. The gross capital required = Project Cost / (1 − fₐ) = $25,000,000 / 0.9625 = $25,974,026. Flotation costs = $974,026. In the NPV analysis, the initial cash outflow is $25,974,026 while projected cash inflows are discounted at the unadjusted WACC of 9.63%. This treatment correctly recognizes flotation costs as a financing cash outflow that reduces the project's NPV without distorting the discount rate used for operating cash flows.",
